<!-- ── the hour ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="band" id="hour">
  <div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="band-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">What actually decides it</span>
      <h2>The first hour is the whole competition.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        In small markets an internship post collects hundreds of applications
        before lunch. Recruiters stop reading long before they stop receiving.
        Being qualified is not what separates you. <strong>Arrival time is.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scroll-driven: the hour fills as you move down the page. -->
    <div class="clock reveal" id="clock">
      <div class="clock-track">
        <div class="clock-fill" id="clockFill"></div>
        <span class="clock-you" id="clockYou">
          <span class="clock-you-dot"></span>
          <span class="clock-you-lbl">You</span>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="clock-ticks">
        <span>0 min</span><span>15</span><span>30</span><span>45</span><span>60 min</span>
      </div>
      <div class="clock-read">
        <div><span class="clock-n tnum" id="clockMin">0</span><span class="clock-k">minutes in</span></div>
        <div><span class="clock-n tnum" id="clockApps">0</span><span class="clock-k">people ahead of you</span></div>
        <div><span class="clock-n" id="clockVerdict">Wide open</span><span class="clock-k">your odds</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── how ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="band alt">
  <div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="band-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Three minutes to set up</span>
      <h2>Then you never open it again.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps-lp">
      <li class="reveal">
        <span class="step-n">01</span>
        <h3>Say what you want</h3>
        <p>Keywords and a country. <em>"intern, software engineer"</em> in Sri Lanka.
           Add as many watches as you like — each runs on its own clock.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal d1">
        <span class="step-n">02</span>
        <h3>It watches, you don't</h3>
        <p>Every few minutes it checks LinkedIn for postings it has never seen.
           No feed to scroll, no tab to keep open, no laptop to leave on.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal d2">
        <span class="step-n">03</span>
        <h3>It only interrupts you when it matters</h3>
        <p>One email per sweep with every new role and a direct apply link.
           Nothing new means silence — which is the point.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── honesty ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="lp-wrap honest reveal">
    <div class="honest-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">The part most tools won't tell you</span>
      <h2>We say <em>minutes</em>, not <em>instantly</em>.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        LinkedIn does not put a job into its search index the moment it is
        published — that takes tens of minutes, and nothing can poll its way
        past it. Anyone promising instant alerts is guessing.
      </p>
      <p class="lead">
        What you get is the rest of the hour. Applicant number nine instead of
        number two hundred. That gap is the entire product, and it is enough.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="honest-figs">
      <div class="fig"><span class="fig-n tnum">&lt;5<small>min</small></span>
        <span class="fig-k">Our part of the delay</span></div>
      <div class="fig"><span class="fig-n tnum">24/7</span>
        <span class="fig-k">Runs while you sleep</span></div>
      <div class="fig"><span class="fig-n tnum">0</span>
        <span class="fig-k">Feeds to check</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

